        {
            "id": "32328386",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "03:29, 1 March 2024",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(10,082 bytes)",
            "change": 34,
            "summary": "(Added and replaced sources for the wikiHaus content team.)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "sourcing",
            "reverted_by": "Eric",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Check the auto settings on your thermostat. If your air conditioner keeps kicking on and off in intervals, look at your thermostat to see if it’s set to “auto” or “automatic.” If it is, this is why your AC keeps shutting off. When it’s set to auto, the AC system will only turn on after the temperature has risen past the number on your thermostat.<ref>https://www.daikinac.com/content/assets/DOC/CTXSWallOperationManual.pdf</ref>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 2 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Change the air filter. Your filter needs to be replaced every 3 months or so. During the hot season, you may need to replace it every month. If you’ve got a problem with your AC unit and you haven’t replaced the filter in a while, do that first. If the problem resolves itself, you don’t need to call anyone. If the problem persists, you’ll probably need to call an HVAC technician.<ref>https://www.energy.gov/energysaver/maintaining-your-air-conditioner</ref>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 4 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Check your vents and ducts for blockages. If there are any obstructions in front of the vents or ducts, this could disrupt your system’s ability to function correctly. Take a stroll around your home and make sure all of your vents and ducts are clear and open. If necessary, rearrange your furniture to make sure that everything is open.<ref>https://www.consumerreports.org/appliances/air-conditioners/troubleshooting-air-conditioner-problems-a5432764698/</ref>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 5 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#See if the air filter is dirty and change it out. Swapping out your air filter may help. A dirty filter can mess with an AC unit’s ability to blow out cool air, so try this first to see the problem resolves itself. If you have a reusable filter, clean it thoroughly before replacing it.<ref>https://www.energy.gov/energysaver/maintaining-your-air-conditioner</ref>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 6 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#It sounds oddly simple, but you turn it off and turn it back on again. Turn your AC off at the thermostat and then use your instruction manual to locate the power button. Press that to shut the AC system down. Then, go to your circuit breaker and flip the breaker for your AC unit off. Wait 1 minute, and turn the breaker back on before restarting your AC unit.<ref>https://www.energy.gov/energysaver/common-air-conditioner-problems</ref>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Yes, even if it seems like there’s nothing wrong. You still need an HVAC technician to come out and service your AC system at least once a year just to keep minor issues from becoming major problems. An HVAC pro will clean your system, check for leaks, and make sure that everything is in working order. This is the best way to make sure that any potential problems get resolved.<ref>https://www.energy.gov/energysaver/common-air-conditioner-problems</ref> [[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 10 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Messing with the electrical components is especially dangerous. AC systems are typically high voltage, and it’s dangerous to toy around or touch any live connections. There are a variety of problems that look like a blown fuse, but aren’t. On top of that, you won’t be able to fix any of these problems yourself, so it’s just going to be more efficient and safe to have a professional take a look.<ref>https://www.energy.gov/energysaver/common-air-conditioner-problems</ref>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 12.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Check the auto settings on your thermostat. If your air conditioner keeps kicking on and off in intervals, look at your thermostat to see if it’s set to “auto” or “automatic.” If it is, this is why your AC keeps shutting off. When it’s set to auto, the AC system will only turn on after the temperature has risen past the number on your thermostat.<ref>http://www.daikinac.com/content/assets/DOC/CTXSWallOperationManual.pdf</ref>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 2 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Change the air filter. Your filter needs to be replaced every 3 months or so. During the hot season, you may need to replace it every month. If you’ve got a problem with your AC unit and you haven’t replaced the filter in a while, do that first. If the problem resolves itself, you don’t need to call anyone. If the problem persists, you’ll probably need to call an HVAC technician.<ref>https://time.com/4408512/air-conditioning-mistakes/</ref>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 4 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Check your vents and ducts for blockages. If there are any obstructions in front of the vents or ducts, this could disrupt your system’s ability to function correctly. Take a stroll around your home and make sure all of your vents and ducts are clear and open. If necessary, rearrange your furniture to make sure that everything is open.<ref>https://www.consumerreports.org/air-conditioners/troubleshooting-air-conditioner-problems/</ref>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 5 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#See if the air filter is dirty and change it out. Swapping out your air filter may help. A dirty filter can mess with an AC unit’s ability to blow out cool air, so try this first to see the problem resolves itself. If you have a reusable filter, clean it thoroughly before replacing it.<ref>https://time.com/4408512/air-conditioning-mistakes/</ref>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 6 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#It sounds oddly simple, but you turn it off and turn it back on again. Turn your AC off at the thermostat and then use your instruction manual to locate the power button. Press that to shut the AC system down. Then, go to your circuit breaker and flip the breaker for your AC unit off. Wait 1 minute, and turn the breaker back on before restarting your AC unit.<ref>https://www.stansac.com/reset-your-thermostat/</ref>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Yes, even if it seems like there’s nothing wrong. You still need an HVAC technician to come out and service your AC system at least once a year just to keep minor issues from becoming major problems. An HVAC pro will clean your system, check for leaks, and make sure that everything is in working order. This is the best way to make sure that any potential problems get resolved.<ref>https://time.com/4408512/air-conditioning-mistakes/</ref> [[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 10 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Messing with the electrical components is especially dangerous. AC systems are typically high voltage, and it’s dangerous to toy around or touch any live connections.<ref>https://americanhomewater.com/ac-blows-a-fuse/</ref> There are a variety of problems that look like a blown fuse, but aren’t. On top of that, you won’t be able to fix any of these problems yourself, so it’s just going to be more efficient and safe to have a professional take a look.<ref>https://www.energy.gov/energysaver/common-air-conditioner-problems</ref>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 12.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
