        {
            "id": "29780756",
            "user": "Wikivisual",
            "timestamp": "15:41, 4 August 2021",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(10,060 bytes)",
            "change": 436,
            "summary": "(Saving new step-by-step photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Check the auto settings on your thermostat. If your air conditioner keeps kicking on and off in intervals, look at your thermostat to see if it’s set to “auto” or “automatic.” If it is, this is why your AC keeps shutting off. When it’s set to auto, the AC system will only turn on after the temperature has risen past the number on your thermostat.<ref>http://www.daikinac.com/content/assets/DOC/CTXSWallOperationManual.pdf</ref>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 2 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Check your vents and ducts for blockages. If there are any obstructions in front of the vents or ducts, this could disrupt your system’s ability to function correctly. Take a stroll around your home and make sure all of your vents and ducts are clear and open. If necessary, rearrange your furniture to make sure that everything is open.<ref>https://www.consumerreports.org/air-conditioners/troubleshooting-air-conditioner-problems/</ref>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 5 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Inspect the refrigerant line. On a central or split system, go outside and look for the largest pipe leading into your AC’s condenser. If your AC is on and this pipe has ice on it, shut your system off and give this pipe time to thaw before turning it back on. If this problem occurs again, you may be running low on refrigerant.<ref>https://youtu.be/beVRELr8Oak?t=30</ref>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 7 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Clean off the condenser coil on the outside unit. Head outside and remove any garbage surrounding the condenser. If anything is stuck in the fins, remove it by hand or with a broom. Then, grab a hose and gently spray down your condenser unit. Keep rinsing it until the water runs clear. Dirty coils can occasionally disrupt the airflow and make it feel like your vents are blowing out warm air.<ref>https://www.bobvila.com/articles/how-to-clean-ac-coils/</ref>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 8.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Messing with the electrical components is especially dangerous. AC systems are typically high voltage, and it’s dangerous to toy around or touch any live connections.<ref>https://americanhomewater.com/ac-blows-a-fuse/</ref> There are a variety of problems that look like a blown fuse, but aren’t. On top of that, you won’t be able to fix any of these problems yourself, so it’s just going to be more efficient and safe to have a professional take a look.<ref>https://www.energy.gov/energysaver/common-air-conditioner-problems</ref>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 12.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Check the auto settings on your thermostat. If your air conditioner keeps kicking on and off in intervals, look at your thermostat to see if it’s set to “auto” or “automatic.” If it is, this is why your AC keeps shutting off. When it’s set to auto, the AC system will only turn on after the temperature has risen past the number on your thermostat.<ref>http://www.daikinac.com/content/assets/DOC/CTXSWallOperationManual.pdf</ref>",
                    "#Check your vents and ducts for blockages. If there are any obstructions in front of the vents or ducts, this could disrupt your system’s ability to function correctly. Take a stroll around your home and make sure all of your vents and ducts are clear and open. If necessary, rearrange your furniture to make sure that everything is open.<ref>https://www.consumerreports.org/air-conditioners/troubleshooting-air-conditioner-problems/</ref>",
                    "#Inspect the refrigerant line. On a central or split system, go outside and look for the largest pipe leading into your AC’s condenser. If your AC is on and this pipe has ice on it, shut your system off and give this pipe time to thaw before turning it back on. If this problem occurs again, you may be running low on refrigerant.<ref>https://youtu.be/beVRELr8Oak?t=30</ref>",
                    "#Clean off the condenser coil on the outside unit. Head outside and remove any garbage surrounding the condenser. If anything is stuck in the fins, remove it by hand or with a broom. Then, grab a hose and gently spray down your condenser unit. Keep rinsing it until the water runs clear. Dirty coils can occasionally disrupt the airflow and make it feel like your vents are blowing out warm air.<ref>https://www.bobvila.com/articles/how-to-clean-ac-coils/</ref>",
                    "#Messing with the electrical components is especially dangerous. AC systems are typically high voltage, and it’s dangerous to toy around or touch any live connections.<ref>https://americanhomewater.com/ac-blows-a-fuse/</ref> There are a variety of problems that look like a blown fuse, but aren’t. On top of that, you won’t be able to fix any of these problems yourself, so it’s just going to be more efficient and safe to have a professional take a look.<ref>https://www.energy.gov/energysaver/common-air-conditioner-problems</ref>"
                ]
            }
        },
