        {
            "id": "29286196",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "18:10, 3 March 2021",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(6,757 bytes)",
            "change": 813,
            "summary": "(Test format for the wikiHaus, please don't revert.)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "revert",
            "reverted_by": "Eric",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "{{fa}}Few things in life are as irritating as your air conditioner acting up on a hot summer day. But before you pick that phone up to spend hard-earned money on a potentially-unnecessary service call, there are a few basic things you should do to see if you can solve or identify the problem yourself. Keep in mind, you will not be able to solve every potential problem. It’s not safe to mess around with your AC’s electrical wiring, and opening the AC system up may just make the problem worse, so don’t hesitate to call for help if you need it.",
                    "==Steps==",
                    "===How do I know if my air conditioner needs to be serviced?===",
                    "#If changing the filter doesn’t help, you probably need a pro. Your filter needs to be replaced every 3 months or so. During the hot season, you may need to replace it every month. If you’ve got a problem with your AC unit and you haven’t replaced the filter in a while, do that first. If the problem resolves itself, you don’t need to call anyone. If the problem persists, you’ll probably need to call an HVAC technician.<ref>https://time.com/4408512/air-conditioning-mistakes/</ref>",
                    "#*[[Clean a Central Air Conditioner|Cleaning your air conditioner]] and replacing the filter should keep most minor problems from getting out of hand.<ref>https://www.bobvila.com/slideshow/11-air-conditioning-mistakes-that-spike-your-bills-51293</ref>",
                    "#*If you’ve got any leaks, electrical problems, or issues keeping your AC system on, you’ll need a service technician to take a look.",
                    "#You should get your AC system serviced once a year for maintenance. Even if everything is working as intended, you should still get an HVAC technician to come out and service your AC system at least once a year. They’ll clean your system, check for leaks, and make sure that everything is in working order. This is the best way to make sure that any potential problems get stopped in their track before things get out of hand.<ref>https://time.com/4408512/air-conditioning-mistakes/</ref>",
                    "===How do I test my air conditioner?===",
                    "#Check the temperature of the air and inspect the refrigerant line. On a central or split system, go outside and look for the largest pipe leading into your AC’s condenser. Let your AC run for a while, and check to see if that pipe has any condensation on it. If it does, your AC is running just fine. You can also set a thermometer inside the return and supply lines on your AC system. After a few minutes, compare the temperature on your thermostat to the thermometer. If they’re relatively close, everything is in working order.<ref>https://youtu.be/beVRELr8Oak?t=30</ref>",
                    "#*A window unit should be pretty easy to test. If the air blowing out matches the temperature you’ve got it set to and there isn’t any ice on the coils, it’s working just fine.",
                    "===How do I reset my air conditioning unit?===",
                    "#It sounds oddly simple, but you turn it off and turn it back on again. Turn your AC off at the thermostat and then use your instruction manual to locate the power button. Press that to shut the AC system down. Then, go to your circuit breaker and flip the breaker for your AC unit off. Wait 1 minute, and turn the breaker back on before restarting your AC unit.<ref>https://www.stansac.com/reset-your-thermostat/</ref>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*It often helps to do this if you accidentally tripped a fuse or you just had a power outage and your AC is acting funny. However, if you have to do this frequently, it’s a sign that something may be going on with your system and you should get an HVAC pro to take a look at it.",
                    "===How do you check an air conditioner fuse?===",
                    "#You really shouldn’t mess around with the fuses on your own. AC systems are typically high voltage, and it’s dangerous to toy around or touch any live connections.<ref>https://americanhomewater.com/ac-blows-a-fuse/</ref> There are a variety of problems that look like a blown fuse, but aren’t. On top of that, you won’t be able to fix any of these problems yourself, so it’s just going to be more efficient and safe to have a professional take a look.<ref>https://www.energy.gov/energysaver/common-air-conditioner-problems</ref>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*You can theoretically check the cartridge fuses on an AC system with a multimeter, but it won’t necessarily tell you much and it’s dangerous. A professional will be able to check the fuses in 30 seconds anyway, so there isn’t a particularly good reason to check them yourself.",
                    "===Why is my air conditioner running but not blowing cold air?===",
                    "#It might be the evaporator coil, refrigerant, or compressor. These problems require a professional eye, but there are a few things you can do first. To start, check your thermostat to make sure the air is actually on and it isn’t just on “auto” or “fan.” Then, try replacing your filter. A clogged filter will often keep cold air from coming out. If the coils are dirty, [[Clean a Coil|cleaning them]] may help as well.<ref>https://www.bobvila.com/articles/air-conditioner-not-cooling/</ref>",
                    "#*On a central air system, check the condensation drain and check the ducts to make sure they’re clear and free of blockages.",
                    "#*If there’s any debris or garbage around your compressor unit, clear it out. This stuff can occasionally interfere with the flow of air.",
                    "===What is the average life of a window air conditioner?===",
                    "#The average window unit will last roughly 8-10 years. If it’s more expensive to repair a window unit than it would be to simply buy a new one, it’s not really worth fixing. Window units don’t last particularly long compared to split or central AC systems, but they’re much cheaper so they’re easier to replace.<ref>https://www.consumerreports.org/window-air-conditioners/when-to-get-a-new-window-air-conditioner/</ref>",
                    "#*For context, a ductless split system will hold up around 15-20 years.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/heating-cooling/21018989/read-this-before-you-buy-ductless-ac</ref> Central air systems typically need to be replaced after 12-17 years.<ref>https://www.bobvila.com/articles/how-long-does-an-air-conditioner-last/</ref>",
                    "{{reflist}}",
                    "__METHODS__"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "{{fa}}Of course, it would be the hottest day of the year that your air conditioner stops working! Getting it serviced can be expensive, and you'll just have to swelter while waiting for a service technician. Or do you? Check out these steps that can possibly save some money, and help you keep your cool at the same time!",
                    "== Steps ==",
                    "",
                    "===Troubleshooting===",
                    "#Assess the problem. Is the unit simply not working at all, is it not cooling the air much, or is it simply blowing around warm air?[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 1 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#It's not working at all: the condenser (for an outdoor unit) or air handler/furnace (indoor unit) are not running.<ref>https://joplins.net/articles/air-conditioner-wont-turn-on-heres-what-to-do</ref>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Make sure it's plugged in. This may seem obvious, but there are occasions when this is exactly the problem. Whether kids, dogs, or an accidental tap with the vacuum cleaner, the plug could be partially or completely dislodged.",
                    "#*Check the circuit breakers and fuses. Make sure they are in place, turned on, and functioning properly. Circuits, especially with older homes, are easily overloaded if the air conditioner shares a circuit with appliances such as irons, refrigerators, or microwaves.",
                    "#*Check your thermostat (cooling control) and make sure it is set to cool at the current temperature, and working properly. As with plugs, sometimes settings can change by accident.",
                    "#Check your fan blower belt for excessive slack, damage, or breakage. This can cause low air flow which allows ice to form, and thus causes low cooling performance.<ref>https://www.hydesac.com/central-air-conditioner-fan-working-potential-explanations/</ref>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Repair or replace the blower belt. Allow the ice to melt before restarting the AC unit.",
                    "#Check the coils and filter. If they are caked with dust and debris, clean them thoroughly. Make sure you let any ice melt before restarting your unit.<ref>https://aristair.com/blog/top-5-causes-frozen-ac-coils/</ref>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Call for service. If you've verified that everything is connected, powered, set up properly, and clean, it may be time to call the service department.[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "===Preventative Maintenance===",
                    "#Wash your condenser coil once a season, or as needed. Turn off your air conditioner, get a garden hose, and spray the coil using an up-and-down pattern. Be particularly attentive if your condenser is close to a dryer vent, or if your neighborhood is full of cottonwood trees. These two items are \"air conditioner killers\".<ref>https://www.energy.gov/energysaver/maintaining-your-air-conditioner</ref>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Clean or change your filter regularly. This simple step alone can increase the efficiency of your air conditioner. It will also run less, increasing the life span of your equipment.<ref>https://www.energy.gov/energysaver/maintaining-your-air-conditioner</ref>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 7 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*A good sign that your filter needs changing is the fact of ice forming inside your inside cooling unit (evaporator section).",
                    "#*This may be difficult to see as the unit is sealed, but touch the outside metal and feel for a noticeable temperature difference. You may also see ice on the large line going to the outside unit (suction line).",
                    "#*If ice is forming, it is possible that your air filter is severely dirty and restricting your normal air flow. This allows ice to form due to the fact that not enough heat is being introduced to your cooling section.",
                    "",
                    "== Video ==",
                    "{{Video:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service|}}",
                    "",
                    "== Tips ==",
                    "*Keep your air conditioner [[Clean an Air Conditioner|clean]] for optimal performance and reliability.",
                    "*Maintaining your air conditioner and having it serviced regularly increases the efficiency of your unit, reduces energy usage, and prevents wear and tear on your equipment.",
                    "",
                    "== Warnings ==",
                    "*Do not attempt to diagnose any electrical problem unless you have been properly trained and/or are licensed.",
                    "*Do not bend the \"fins\" on the outside of the condenser.  Make certain to wash the coils in an \"up/down\" fashion.",
                    "*Do not attempt any repair work unless you are a licensed heating and air conditioning technician.  Most states require a licensed heating and air conditioning contractor to perform any repair work.",
                    "*Do not immediately assume that if your air conditioner is not working that the thermostat is at fault.  Tampering with the thermostat or is related wiring can cause more problems than you initially started with.  If nothing is happening with the thermostat set, immediately call for service.",
                    "*When in doubt, stop.  Immediately call a licensed heating and air conditioning contractor.",
                    "*Do not attempt to add or remove refrigerant from your system unless you are licensed by the EPA.  Fines and penalties await those who tamper with or release refrigerant in an unsafe or unapproved fashion.",
                    "",
                    "== Things You'll Need ==",
                    "*Garden hose",
                    "*Replacement filter",
                    "*Telephone book",
                    "*Patience",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "{{reflist}}",
                    ""
                ]
            }
        },
