        {
            "id": "13088479",
            "user": "Serendipitee",
            "timestamp": "06:43, 26 March 2014",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(6,671 bytes)",
            "change": 5000,
            "summary": "(Reverted edits by 27.124.34.198 (Talk); changed back to last version by Votebot)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "revert",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "{{fa}}Of course, it would be the hottest day of the year that your air conditioner stops working! Getting it serviced can be expensive, and you'll just have to swelter while waiting for a service technician. Or do you? Check out these steps that can possibly save some money, and help you keep your cool at the same time!",
                    "[[Category:Air Conditioning]]",
                    "== Steps ==",
                    "===Troubleshooting===",
                    "#Assess the problem. Is the unit simply not working at all, is it not cooling the air much, or is it simply blowing around warm air?<br><br>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#It's not working at all: the condenser (for an outdoor unit) or air handler/furnace (indoor unit) are not running.<br><br>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*Make sure it's plugged in. This may seem obvious, but there are occasions when this is exactly the problem. Whether kids, dogs, or an accidental tap with the vacuum cleaner, the plug could be partially or completely dislodged.<br><br>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 2Bullet1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*Check the circuit breakers and fuses. Make sure they are in place, turned on, and functioning properly. Circuits, especially with older homes, are easily overloaded if the air conditioner shares a circuit with appliances such as irons, refrigerators, or microwaves.<br><br>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 2Bullet2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*Check your thermostat (cooling control) and make sure it is set to cool at the current temperature, and working properly. As with plugs, sometimes settings can change by accident.<br><br>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 2Bullet3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Check your fan blower belt for excessive slack, damage, or breakage. This can cause low air flow which allows ice to form, and thus causes low cooling performance.<br><br>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*Repair or replace the blower belt. Allow the ice to melt before restarting the AC unit.",
                    "#Check the coils and filter. If they are caked with dust and debris, clean them thoroughly. Make sure you let any ice melt before restarting your unit.<br><br>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 4.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Call for service. If you've verified that everything is connected, powered, set up properly, and clean, it may be time to call the service department.<br><br>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 5.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "===Preventative Maintenance===",
                    "#Wash your condenser coil once a season, or as needed. Turn off your air conditioner, get a garden hose, and spray the coil using an up-and-down pattern. Be particularly attentive if your condenser is close to a dryer vent, or if your neighborhood is full of cottonwood trees. These two items are \"air conditioner killers\".<br><br>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 6.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Clean or change your filter regularly. This simple step alone can increase the efficiency of your air conditioner. It will also run less, increasing the life span of your equipment.<br><br>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 7.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*A good sign that your filter needs changing is the fact of ice forming inside your inside cooling unit (evaporator section).",
                    "#*This may be difficult to see as the unit is sealed, but touch the outside metal and feel for a noticeable temperature difference. You may also see ice on the large line going to the outside unit (suction line).",
                    "#*If ice is forming, it is possible that your air filter is severely dirty and restricting your normal air flow. This allows ice to form due to the fact that not enough heat is being introduced to your cooling section.",
                    "#*This allows the low side pressure to fall, which is linked to low operating pressure. When the temperature falls below freezing, the condensate water (which collects on the evaporator normally) will start to freeze. Once started, a significant amount of ice will form rapidly, compounding the problem of low discharge airflow to the cooling space, causing low cooling performance and possible damage to the compressor.",
                    "== Video ==",
                    "{{Video:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service|}}",
                    "== Tips ==",
                    "*Maintaining your air conditioner and having it serviced regularly increases the efficiency of your unit, reduces energy usage, and prevents wear and tear on your equipment.",
                    "*Keep your air conditioner [[Clean an Air Conditioner|clean]] for optimal performance and reliability.",
                    "",
                    "== Warnings ==",
                    "*Do not bend the \"fins\" on the outside of the condenser.  Make certain to wash the coils in an \"up/down\" fashion.",
                    "*Do not attempt to diagnose any electrical problem unless you have been properly trained and/or are licensed.",
                    "*Do not attempt any repair work unless you are a licensed heating and air conditioning technician.  Most states require a licensed heating and air conditioning contractor to perform any repair work.",
                    "*Do not attempt to add or remove refrigerant from your system unless you are licensed by the EPA.  Fines and penalties await those who tamper with or release refrigerant in an unsafe or unapproved fashion.",
                    "*Do not immediately assume that if your air conditioner is not working that the thermostat is at fault.  Tampering with the thermostat or is related wiring can cause more problems than you initially started with.  If nothing is happening with the thermostat set, immediately call for service.",
                    "*When in doubt, stop.  Immediately call a licensed heating and air conditioning contractor.",
                    "",
                    "== Things You'll Need ==",
                    "*Garden hose",
                    "*Replacement filter",
                    "*Telephone book",
                    "*Patience",
                    "",
                    "== Related wikiHows ==",
                    "*[[Check a Thermostat in a Dryer|How to Check a Thermostat in a Dryer]]",
                    "*[[Build Your Own Air Conditioner|How to Build Your Own Air Conditioner]]",
                    "*[[Cool Yourself Without Air Conditioning|How to Cool Yourself Without Air Conditioning]]",
                    "*[[Choose an Air Compressor|How to Choose an Air Compressor]]",
                    "*[[Keep the Upstairs of Your Air Conditioned Home Cooler|How to Keep the Upstairs of Your Air Conditioned Home Cooler]]",
                    "*[[Make a Water Heater|How to Make a Water Heater]]",
                    "*[[Troubleshoot a Heat Pump or Air Conditioning System|How to Troubleshoot a Heat Pump or Air Conditioning System]]",
                    "",
                    "[[es:revisar el aire acondicionado antes de llamar para hacerle servicio]]",
                    "[[it:Controllare l'Aria Condizionata Prima di Chiamare il Tecnico]]",
                    "[[de:Sich die Klimaanlage ansehen bevor man den Reparaturdienst holt]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "a lot of homes have gas heat. For a gas furnace, the gas meter needs to be in place and your gas account active. Five or six times every year, we find out that someone moves into a house, but has never called for gas service. This occurs mostly when someone moves from a warm climate  where there is very little heat needed.",
                    "http://gurgaonrefrigeration.com/",
                    "We as a reputed home appliances AC, Air Conditioner repair company in Gurgaon (Delhi-ncr). We provide all kind of Air Conditioner and Air Condition services in Gurgaon. Call us at 08860831751 for support.",
                    "We provide services in Gurgaon NCR, Refrigerator Services in Gurgaon, Refrigerator installation in Gurgaon, Single Door Air Conditioner in Gurgaon, Double Door – Top Freezer Air Conditioner in Gurgaon, Side-by-Side Door Air Conditioner in Gurgaon, Double Door- Bottom Freezer Air Conditioner in Gurgaon, French Door Air Conditioner in Gurgaon, Triple Door Air Conditioner in Gurgaon.",
                    "We Repair all brands of AC with quality and satisfaction of our consumer. And we are known as AC Repair Services in Gurgaon and Delhi/NCR. We, ‘Gurgaon Refrigeration' are a reputed AC installation and repair service providers. Tested and approved, our range of AC repair service is appreciated for its durability and easy operations by our expert technicians. Our skilled professionals, the warmth of personalized attention and consistency of repair service will keep you coming back to us.",
                    "We have been able to gain trust and confidence of esteemed clients. We provide AC repair service in Faridabad, Delhi, Sohna Road and Gurgaon.",
                    "We are Service Provided these all City.http://gurgaonrefrigeration.com/sitemap.html"
                ]
            }
        },
