        {
            "id": "11623116",
            "user": "Wikiphoto",
            "timestamp": "10:36, 31 October 2013",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(6,687 bytes)",
            "change": 89,
            "summary": "(Enlarging and centering Steps photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "reverted_by": "Joel.Miles925",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Assess the problem. Is the unit simply not working at all, is it not cooling the air much, or is it simply blowing around warm air?<br><br>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#It's not working at all: the condenser (for an outdoor unit) or air handler/furnace (indoor unit) are not running.<br><br>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*Make sure it's plugged in. This may seem obvious, but there are occasions when this is exactly the problem. Whether kids, dogs, or an accidental tap with the vacuum cleaner, the plug could be partially or completely dislodged.<br><br>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 2Bullet1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*Check the circuit breakers and fuses. Make sure they are in place, turned on, and functioning properly. Circuits, especially with older homes, are easily overloaded if the air conditioner shares a circuit with appliances such as irons, refrigerators, or microwaves.<br><br>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 2Bullet2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*Check your thermostat (cooling control) and make sure it is set to cool at the current temperature, and working properly. As with plugs, sometimes settings can change by accident.<br><br>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 2Bullet3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Check your fan blower belt for excessive slack, damage, or breakage. This can cause low air flow which allows ice to form, and thus causes low cooling performance.<br><br>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Check the coils and filter. If they are caked with dust and debris, clean them thoroughly. Make sure you let any ice melt before restarting your unit.<br><br>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 4.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Call for service. If you've verified that everything is connected, powered, set up properly, and clean, it may be time to call the service department.<br><br>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 5.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Wash your condenser coil once a season, or as needed. Turn off your air conditioner, get a garden hose, and spray the coil using an up-and-down pattern. Be particularly attentive if your condenser is close to a dryer vent, or if your neighborhood is full of cottonwood trees. These two items are \"air conditioner killers\".<br><br>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 6.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Clean or change your filter regularly. This simple step alone can increase the efficiency of your air conditioner. It will also run less, increasing the life span of your equipment.<br><br>[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 7.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    ""
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 1.jpg|right|300px]]Assess the problem. Is the unit simply not working at all, is it not cooling the air much, or is it simply blowing around warm air?",
                    "#[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 2.jpg|right|300px]]It's not working at all: the condenser (for an outdoor unit) or air handler/furnace (indoor unit) are not running.",
                    "#*[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 2Bullet1.jpg|right|300px]]Make sure it's plugged in. This may seem obvious, but there are occasions when this is exactly the problem. Whether kids, dogs, or an accidental tap with the vacuum cleaner, the plug could be partially or completely dislodged.",
                    "#*[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 2Bullet2.jpg|right|300px]]Check the circuit breakers and fuses. Make sure they are in place, turned on, and functioning properly. Circuits, especially with older homes, are easily overloaded if the air conditioner shares a circuit with appliances such as irons, refrigerators, or microwaves.",
                    "#*[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 2Bullet3.jpg|right|300px]]Check your thermostat (cooling control) and make sure it is set to cool at the current temperature, and working properly. As with plugs, sometimes settings can change by accident.",
                    "#[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 3.jpg|right|300px]]Check your fan blower belt for excessive slack, damage, or breakage. This can cause low air flow which allows ice to form, and thus causes low cooling performance.",
                    "#[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 4.jpg|right|300px]]Check the coils and filter. If they are caked with dust and debris, clean them thoroughly. Make sure you let any ice melt before restarting your unit.",
                    "#[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 5.jpg|right|300px]]Call for service. If you've verified that everything is connected, powered, set up properly, and clean, it may be time to call the service department.",
                    "",
                    "#[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 6.jpg|right|300px]]Wash your condenser coil once a season, or as needed. Turn off your air conditioner, get a garden hose, and spray the coil using an up-and-down pattern. Be particularly attentive if your condenser is close to a dryer vent, or if your neighborhood is full of cottonwood trees. These two items are \"air conditioner killers\".",
                    "#[[Image:Check Your Air Conditioner Before Calling for Service Step 7.jpg|right|300px]]Clean or change your filter regularly. This simple step alone can increase the efficiency of your air conditioner. It will also run less, increasing the life span of your equipment."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "11623114",
