            "id": "8843366",
            "user": "LargeImageBot",
            "timestamp": "18:11, 10 December 2012",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(5,761 bytes)",
            "change": -80,
            "summary": "(Resized images to the {{largeimage}} size.)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "deletion",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#*Check your thermostat (cooling control) and make sure it is set to cool at the current temperature, and working properly. As with plugs, sometimes settings can change by accident.{{largeimage|CheckThermostat Step 1.jpg}}",
                    "#*Repair or replace the blower belt. Allow the ice to melt before restarting the AC unit.{{largeimage|CheckFanBlower Step 3.jpg}}",
                    "#Call for service. If you've verified that everything is connected, powered, set up properly, and clean, it may be time to call the service department.{{largeimage|Turnoff Step 5.jpg}}",
                    "#Wash your condenser coil once a season, or as needed. Turn off your air conditioner, get a garden hose, and spray the coil using an up-and-down pattern. Be particularly attentive if your condenser is close to a dryer vent, or if your neighborhood is full of cottonwood trees. These two items are \"air conditioner killers\".{{largeimage|WashCondeser Step 4.jpg}}",
                    "#*This allows the low side pressure to fall, which is linked to low operating pressure. When the temperature falls below freezing, the condensate water (which collects on the evaporator normally) will start to freeze. Once started, a significant amount of ice will form rapidly, compounding the problem of low discharge airflow to the cooling space, causing low cooling performance and possible damage to the compressor.{{largeimage|ChangeFilter Step 2.jpg}}"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#*Check your thermostat (cooling control) and make sure it is set to cool at the current temperature, and working properly. As with plugs, sometimes settings can change by accident.<br><br>[[Image:CheckThermostat Step 1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*Repair or replace the blower belt. Allow the ice to melt before restarting the AC unit.<br><br>[[Image:CheckFanBlower Step 3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Call for service. If you've verified that everything is connected, powered, set up properly, and clean, it may be time to call the service department.<br><br>[[Image:Turnoff Step 5.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Wash your condenser coil once a season, or as needed. Turn off your air conditioner, get a garden hose, and spray the coil using an up-and-down pattern. Be particularly attentive if your condenser is close to a dryer vent, or if your neighborhood is full of cottonwood trees. These two items are \"air conditioner killers\".<br><br>[[Image:WashCondeser Step 4.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*This allows the low side pressure to fall, which is linked to low operating pressure. When the temperature falls below freezing, the condensate water (which collects on the evaporator normally) will start to freeze. Once started, a significant amount of ice will form rapidly, compounding the problem of low discharge airflow to the cooling space, causing low cooling performance and possible damage to the compressor.<br><br>[[Image:ChangeFilter Step 2.jpg|center|550px]]"
                ]
            }
