        {
            "id": "8612127",
            "user": "Chris H",
            "timestamp": "22:41, 21 October 2012",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(5,767 bytes)",
            "change": 1403,
            "summary": "(revert)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "revert",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "",
                    "===Troubleshooting===",
                    "#Assess the problem. Is the unit simply not working at all, is it not cooling the air much, or is it simply blowing around warm air?",
                    "#It's not working at all: the condenser (for an outdoor unit) or air handler/furnace (indoor unit) are not running.",
                    "#*Make sure it's plugged in. This may seem obvious, but there are occasions when this is exactly the problem. Whether kids, dogs, or an accidental tap with the vacuum cleaner, the plug could be partially or completely dislodged.",
                    "#*Check the circuit breakers and fuses. Make sure they are in place, turned on, and functioning properly. Circuits, especially with older homes, are easily overloaded if the air conditioner shares a circuit with appliances such as irons, refrigerators, or microwaves.",
                    "#*Check your thermostat (cooling control) and make sure it is set to cool at the current temperature, and working properly. As with plugs, sometimes settings can change by accident.<br><br>[[Image:CheckThermostat Step 1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Check your fan blower belt for excessive slack, damage, or breakage. This can cause low air flow which allows ice to form, and thus causes low cooling performance.",
                    "#*Repair or replace the blower belt. Allow the ice to melt before restarting the AC unit.<br><br>[[Image:CheckFanBlower Step 3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Check the coils and filter. If they are caked with dust and debris, clean them thoroughly. Make sure you let any ice melt before restarting your unit.",
                    "#Call for service. If you've verified that everything is connected, powered, set up properly, and clean, it may be time to call the service department.<br><br>[[Image:Turnoff Step 5.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "",
                    "===Preventative Maintenance===",
                    "#Wash your condenser coil once a season, or as needed. Turn off your air conditioner, get a garden hose, and spray the coil using an up-and-down pattern. Be particularly attentive if your condenser is close to a dryer vent, or if your neighborhood is full of cottonwood trees. These two items are \"air conditioner killers\".<br><br>[[Image:WashCondeser Step 4.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Clean or change your filter regularly. This simple step alone can increase the efficiency of your air conditioner. It will also run less, increasing the life span of your equipment.",
                    "#*A good sign that your filter needs changing is the fact of ice forming inside your inside cooling unit (evaporator section).",
                    "#*This may be difficult to see as the unit is sealed, but touch the outside metal and feel for a noticeable temperature difference. You may also see ice on the large line going to the outside unit (suction line).",
                    "#*If ice is forming, it is possible that your air filter is severely dirty and restricting your normal air flow. This allows ice to form due to the fact that not enough heat is being introduced to your cooling section.",
                    "#*This allows the low side pressure to fall, which is linked to low operating pressure. When the temperature falls below freezing, the condensate water (which collects on the evaporator normally) will start to freeze. Once started, a significant amount of ice will form rapidly, compounding the problem of low discharge airflow to the cooling space, causing low cooling performance and possible damage to the compressor.<br><br>[[Image:ChangeFilter Step 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "*Keep your air conditioner [[Clean an Air Conditioner|clean]] for optimal performance and reliability.",
                    "*Do not attempt any repair work unless you are a licensed heating and air conditioning technician.  Most states require a licensed heating and air conditioning contractor to perform any repair work.",
                    "*Do not attempt to add or remove refrigerant from your system unless you are licensed by the EPA.  Fines and penalties await those who tamper with or release refrigerant in an unsafe or unapproved fashion.",
                    "*Do not attempt to diagnose any electrical problem unless you have been properly trained and/or are licensed.",
                    "*Do not immediately assume that if your air conditioner is not working that the thermostat is at fault.  Tampering with the thermostat or is related wiring can cause more problems than you initially started with.  If nothing is happening with the thermostat set, immediately call for service.",
                    "*Do not bend the \"fins\" on the outside of the condenser.  Make certain to wash the coils in an \"up/down\" fashion.",
                    "*When in doubt, stop.  Immediately call a licensed heating and air conditioning contractor.",
                    "*Garden hose",
                    "*Replacement filter",
                    "*Telephone book",
                    "*Patience",
                    "*[[Check a Thermostat in a Dryer|How to Check a Thermostat in a Dryer]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Check your thermostat and make sure it is set to cool at the appropriate temperature. Replace the batteries in your thermostat if needed.<br><br>[[Image:CheckThermostat Step 1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Look at the outlet to make sure that the unit is plugged in. Doing this may seem basic, but sometimes your kids, your vacuum cleaner or your lawnmower can knock the plug out of the outlet.",
                    "#Examine the circuit breakers and fuses that control your air conditioner. Make sure they are in place, turned on, and functioning properly. Circuits, especially with older homes, are easily overloaded if your air conditioner shares a circuit with appliances such as irons, refrigerators or microwaves.",
                    "#Find the disconnect switch next to your outdoor unit. The switch is usually located in a large gray box. Make sure the switch is set to \"on.\"",
                    "#Change or clean your filter. You should change your filter monthly to ensure proper air conditioner functioning.<br><br>[[Image:ChangeFilter Step 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Check the grilles and air supply registers. Move any furniture or drapes that are blocking the grilles and registers.<ref>http://www.reliantairconditioning.com/what-do-you-call-air-conditioner-dallas/</ref>",
                    "#Look for water around your air conditioning unit. If you have a leak, then check your drainage tubes for ice, debris or algae. Cleaning the tubes should prevent further leakage.<ref>http://www.servicemagic.com/article.show.Air-Conditioning-Repair-When-to-Call-in-a-Professional.14197.html</ref>",
                    "#Clean outdoor fins and coils. If your outdoor unit is clogged with dust and debris, then you should take steps to [[Clean an Air Conditioner| clean your air conditioner]] thoroughly.<br><br>[[Image:WashCondeser Step 4.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Check your fan blower belt for excessive slack, damage or breakage. Belt problems can cause low air flow, allowing ice to form and inhibiting cooling performance. Repair or replace the blower belt, and allow any ice to melt before restarting the A/C unit.<br><br>[[Image:CheckFanBlower Step 3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Call for service if none of these steps works. Ask for recommendations from friends and family. You can also check consumer forums, consumer advocacy websites or the Better Business Bureau to find a reputable company to service your air conditioner.",
                    "*If your condenser unit is located near a dryer vent, or if your neighborhood is full of cottonwood trees, then you should check your condenser often for blockages.",
                    "*Do not attempt any major repair work unless you are a licensed heating and air conditioning technician.  Most states require a licensed heating and air conditioning contractor to perform any repair work.",
                    "*Avoid adding or removing refrigerant from your system unless you are licensed by the EPA.  If you tamper with or release refrigerant in an unsafe or unapproved fashion, you could face severe penalties and fines.",
                    "*Rely on your electrician. Let the pros diagnose electrical problems so that you don't end up severely damaging your home's electrical system or injuring yourself and others.",
                    "*Batteries for your thermostat",
                    "*New filter",
                    "",
                    "==Sources and Citations==",
                    "{{reflist}}"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "8597010",
            "user": "JackieL33",
            "timestamp": "17:42, 18 October 2012",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(4,364 bytes)",
            "change": 269,
            "summary": "(Changed the intro, added an image, softened the wording in the Warnings section, formatted sources)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "sourcing",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "Of course, it would be the hottest day of the year that your air conditioner stops working! Getting it serviced can be expensive, and you'll just have to swelter while waiting for a service technician. Or do you? Check out these steps that can possibly save some money, and help you keep your cool at the same time!",
                    "# Look at the outlet to make sure that the unit is plugged in. Doing this may seem basic, but sometimes your kids, your vacuum cleaner or your lawnmower can knock the plug out of the outlet.",
                    "#Check the grilles and air supply registers. Move any furniture or drapes that are blocking the grilles and registers.<ref>http://www.reliantairconditioning.com/what-do-you-call-air-conditioner-dallas/</ref>",
                    "#Look for water around your air conditioning unit. If you have a leak, then check your drainage tubes for ice, debris or algae. Cleaning the tubes should prevent further leakage.<ref>http://www.servicemagic.com/article.show.Air-Conditioning-Repair-When-to-Call-in-a-Professional.14197.html</ref>",
                    "#Check your fan blower belt for excessive slack, damage or breakage. Belt problems can cause low air flow, allowing ice to form and inhibiting cooling performance. Repair or replace the blower belt, and allow any ice to melt before restarting the A/C unit.<br><br>[[Image:CheckFanBlower Step 3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "*Do not attempt any major repair work unless you are a licensed heating and air conditioning technician.  Most states require a licensed heating and air conditioning contractor to perform any repair work.",
                    "*Avoid adding or removing refrigerant from your system unless you are licensed by the EPA.  If you tamper with or release refrigerant in an unsafe or unapproved fashion, you could face severe penalties and fines.",
                    "*Rely on your electrician. Let the pros diagnose electrical problems so that you don't end up severely damaging your home's electrical system or injuring yourself and others.",
                    "{{reflist}}"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "The last thing you need on a sweltering summer day is for your air conditioner to stop working. At the same time, placing a service call can be quite expensive, and you may have to wait days before a technician can fix your air conditioner. Try these steps to check your air conditioner before calling for service.",
                    "#Check the grilles and air supply registers. Move any furniture or drapes that are blocking the grilles and registers.",
                    "#Look for water around your air conditioning unit. If you have a leak, then check your drainage tubes for ice, debris or algae. Cleaning the tubes should prevent further leakage.",
                    "#Check your fan blower belt for excessive slack, damage or breakage. Belt problems can cause low air flow, allowing ice to form and inhibiting cooling performance. Repair or replace the blower belt, and allow any ice to melt before restarting the A/C unit.",
                    "*Do not attempt any repair work unless you are a licensed heating and air conditioning technician.  Most states require a licensed heating and air conditioning contractor to perform any repair work.",
                    "*Do not attempt to add or remove refrigerant from your system unless you are licensed by the EPA.  Fines and penalties await those who tamper with or release refrigerant in an unsafe or unapproved fashion.",
                    "*Do not attempt to diagnose any electrical problem unless you have been properly trained and/or are licensed.",
                    "*[http://www.reliantairconditioning.com/what-do-you-call-air-conditioner-dallas/ What to Do Before You Call]",
                    "*[http://www.servicemagic.com/article.show.Air-Conditioning-Repair-When-to-Call-in-a-Professional.14197.html Air Conditioning Repair: When to Call in a Professional]"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "8469643",
            "user": "Zandra28",
            "timestamp": "17:22, 23 September 2012",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(4,095 bytes)",
            "change": -1715,
            "summary": "(Condensed the article for better clarity, added some steps for DIY repair)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_deletion",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "The last thing you need on a sweltering summer day is for your air conditioner to stop working. At the same time, placing a service call can be quite expensive, and you may have to wait days before a technician can fix your air conditioner. Try these steps to check your air conditioner before calling for service.",
                    "#Check your thermostat and make sure it is set to cool at the appropriate temperature. Replace the batteries in your thermostat if needed.<br><br>[[Image:CheckThermostat Step 1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Examine the circuit breakers and fuses that control your air conditioner. Make sure they are in place, turned on, and functioning properly. Circuits, especially with older homes, are easily overloaded if your air conditioner shares a circuit with appliances such as irons, refrigerators or microwaves.",
                    "#Find the disconnect switch next to your outdoor unit. The switch is usually located in a large gray box. Make sure the switch is set to \"on.\"",
                    "#Change or clean your filter. You should change your filter monthly to ensure proper air conditioner functioning.<br><br>[[Image:ChangeFilter Step 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Check the grilles and air supply registers. Move any furniture or drapes that are blocking the grilles and registers.",
                    "#Look for water around your air conditioning unit. If you have a leak, then check your drainage tubes for ice, debris or algae. Cleaning the tubes should prevent further leakage.",
                    "#Clean outdoor fins and coils. If your outdoor unit is clogged with dust and debris, then you should take steps to [[Clean an Air Conditioner| clean your air conditioner]] thoroughly.<br><br>[[Image:WashCondeser Step 4.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Check your fan blower belt for excessive slack, damage or breakage. Belt problems can cause low air flow, allowing ice to form and inhibiting cooling performance. Repair or replace the blower belt, and allow any ice to melt before restarting the A/C unit.",
                    "#Call for service if none of these steps works. Ask for recommendations from friends and family. You can also check consumer forums, consumer advocacy websites or the Better Business Bureau to find a reputable company to service your air conditioner.",
                    "*If your condenser unit is located near a dryer vent, or if your neighborhood is full of cottonwood trees, then you should check your condenser often for blockages.",
                    "*Batteries for your thermostat",
                    "*New filter",
                    "",
                    "==Sources and Citations==",
                    "*[http://www.reliantairconditioning.com/what-do-you-call-air-conditioner-dallas/ What to Do Before You Call]",
                    "*[http://www.servicemagic.com/article.show.Air-Conditioning-Repair-When-to-Call-in-a-Professional.14197.html Air Conditioning Repair: When to Call in a Professional]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "[[Image:CheckAircon Intro.jpg|right|300px]]Of course, it would be the hottest day of the year that your air conditioner stops working! Getting it serviced can be expensive, and you'll just have to swelter while waiting for a service technician. Or do you? Check out these steps that can possibly save some money, and help you keep your cool at the same time!",
